// Who lived where in 2024.
//
// Two tables. `countries` is the composition of a whole country; `regions` overrides it for
// the places where a national average is a lie - Tatarstan is not Russia's average, and
// Xinjiang is not China's.
//
// A culture may name the regions it actually lives in:
//
//   "uyghur": { "share": 0.008, "where": [ "CN-XJ" ] }
//
// Without that line a minority is smeared evenly over the whole country, which is how a
// game ends up with Tibetans in Shanghai. With it, the whole national head-count is placed
// in the regions named, in proportion to how big they are, and everyone else fills what is
// left. It is one line, and it is the difference between a map worth looking at and a map
// that is the same colour everywhere.
//
// `primary_cultures` says whose state it is. Everyone else in it is a minority, which is
// what assimilation works on and what radicalises a region when its people are governed by
// somebody they are not. Left unsaid, the largest culture is taken to be the state's own.
//
// Shares are of the whole country and are normalised, so they need not add to exactly one.
// Figures are 2020s censuses and estimates, rounded: the point is the shape of a country,
// not its third decimal place.
